$ =========================================================================
$ cessna210_flagship_bulk.bdf — FLAGSHIP realistic-airplane SOL 144 bulk model
$ (Step 65).  Bulk data ONLY — no case control.  Pulled in by the three thin
$ drivers via a case-control INCLUDE:
$
$   cessna210_flagship_trim.bdf     SC1 1g cruise + SC2 2.5g pull-up
$   cessna210_flagship_massset.bdf  one TRIM x three MASSSET payload cases
$   cessna210_flagship_mloads.bdf   trim + elevator-ramp transient (MLOADS)
$
$ Companion section-coefficient table: cessna210_flagship_section_data.csv.
$ Tutorial walk-through: docs/20_theory/02_realistic_airplane_sol144.md.
$
$ Coordinate system: X = aft along the fuselage, Y = starboard, Z = up;
$ origin ahead of the spinner.  Units: SI (m, N, kg, Pa, s).
$
$ Full-span model (SYMXZ = 0): both wing halves and both HTP halves are
$ modelled explicitly; the VTP lies in the XZ plane (y = 0) as one CAERO1.
$ sbeam has no half-span/symmetry support — see AEROS below.
$
$ -------------------------------------------------------------------------
$ AIRCRAFT GEOMETRY (Cessna 210-like, cantilever high wing)
$ -------------------------------------------------------------------------
$   Wing:  span 11.20, root chord 1.75, tip chord 1.15, area 16.24,
$          MAC 1.4707, AR 7.723, 1.5 deg dihedral, high wing at z = 1.25.
$          Root LE at (2.00, 0, 1.25); tip LE at (2.15, 5.60, 1.397).
$          The QUARTER-CHORD LINE IS STRAIGHT at x = 2.4375 (root
$          2.00 + 0.4375, tip 2.15 + 0.2875), so the quarter-MAC is at
$          x = 2.4375 exactly.  y_MAC = 2.607, LE of the MAC at x = 2.0698.
$          (An earlier revision of this model quoted "quarter-MAC near
$          x = 2.55" — that was wrong by 0.11 m; the value above is exact.)
$   HTP:   span 3.46, root chord 1.05, tip chord 0.75, at z = 0.70.
$          Root LE at (6.90, 0, 0.70); tip LE at (7.10, 1.73, 0.70).
$          The TWO-THIRDS-CHORD LINE IS ALSO STRAIGHT, at x = 7.60
$          (root 6.90 + 0.70, tip 7.10 + 0.50) — this is the elevator hinge.
$          Area 3.114, quarter-MAC at x = 7.2216, tail arm 4.784,
$          tail volume V_H = 3.114 x 4.784 / (16.24 x 1.4707) = 0.6237.
$   VTP:   height 1.50, root chord 1.50, tip chord 0.85.
$          Root LE at (6.60, 0, 0.80), tip LE at (7.10, 0, 2.30).
$ =========================================================================
$
$ ----------------------------------------------------------------- MATERIAL
$ Aluminium alloy (2024-T3 representative): E = 70 GPa, G = 26.9 GPa,
$ nu = 0.3, rho = 2700.  The MAT1 density is live: CBAR distributed mass
$ (rho*A) carries the primary STRUCTURE and the CONM2s carry the powerplant,
$ systems, furnishing and payload.  See the mass budget below.
MAT1, 1, 7.0e10, 2.69e10, 0.3, 2700.0
$
$ ---------------------------------------------------------------- PROPERTIES
$ Cross-section areas are sized as a MASS model as well as a stiffness model:
$ rho*A*L over each group must land on a believable structural weight.  (The
$ pre-flagship revision of this deck used A = 0.020 on the fuselage, which is
$ a ~54 kg/m spine — 564 kg of structure at CG x = 4.06.  That is a stiffness
$ placeholder, not an airframe, and it made the target CG unreachable.)
$
$ PID 1 — forward fuselage (x 0.50 -> 5.00)   A=0.0100  m_struct = 121.5 kg
PBAR, 1, 1, 0.0100, 0.010, 0.010, 0.015
$ PID 4 — aft fuselage / tailcone (x 5.00 -> 8.00), tapered  A=0.0040
PBAR, 4, 1, 0.0040, 0.0040, 0.0040, 0.0060
$ PID 2 — wing spar box.  MIND THE PBAR PLANES: I1 is about the element
$ y-axis and I2 about the element z-axis, so for a wing CBAR running along
$ global Y with orientation vector (1,0,0) it is I2 that resists FLAPWISE
$ (vertical) bending and I1 that resists CHORDWISE bending — the opposite of
$ what the pre-flagship revision of this deck claimed in its comment.
$ Verified directly: dividing I2 by 10 multiplies the 1g wing-tip deflection
$ by 10 (13.5 -> 134.7 mm); dividing I1 by 10 changes it by 0.15%.
$
$ I2 and J are CALIBRATED, not inherited.  With the pre-flagship values
$ (I2=8.0e-5, J=3.0e-4) the wing is effectively rigid: the elastic-restrained
$ CZ_alpha sits +0.44% off rigid and divergence is 175x the trim q, so there
$ is no aeroelasticity left to demonstrate.  The values below put the
$ elastic-restrained CZ_alpha +4.1% above rigid with divergence still 19.8x
$ the cruise q, and give wing-tip deflections of 109 mm at 1g / 272 mm at
$ 2.5g (2.0% / 4.9% of the 5.6 m semispan) — a believable GA wing.
$   A=0.0050, I1 (chordwise), I2 (flapwise), J (torsion)
PBAR, 2, 1, 0.0050, 8.0e-4, 1.0e-5, 3.0e-5
$ PID 3 — empennage box (HTP/VTP)   A=0.00225
PBAR, 3, 1, 0.00225, 2.0e-4, 2.0e-5, 1.0e-4
$
$ ------------------------------------------------------------ COORD SYSTEMS
$ CID 990 — stability axis referenced by AEROS RCSID: origin at the trim
$ reference point GRID 900 (the quarter-MAC), z DOWN.  This is the HA144A
$ convention, and it is what makes URDD3 = -g a 1g pull (see the TRIM cards).
CORD2R, 990, 0, 2.4375, 0.0, 0.60, 2.4375, 0.0, -9.40
+, 12.4375, 0.0, 0.60
$
$ CIDs 991-994 — SPLINE2 axis systems.  MSC convention: the spline axis is
$ the CID *y*-axis, the x-axis is the chord / rigid-arm direction and the
$ z-axis the deflection direction.  Each is built from the elastic axis it
$ serves: y = unit(root EA -> tip EA), z = global +Z made orthogonal to y
$ (so the 1.5 deg wing dihedral is carried exactly), x = y cross z.
$ CID 991 — RIGHT wing spline axis, y = (-0.016064, 0.999527, 0.026238)
CORD2R, 991, 0, 2.70000, 0.00000, 1.25000, 2.70422, -0.26234, 11.24656
+, 12.69871, 0.16069, 1.25000
$ CID 992 — LEFT wing spline axis (mirror), y = (-0.016064, -0.999527, 0.026238)
CORD2R, 992, 0, 2.70000, 0.00000, 1.25000, 2.70422, 0.26234, 11.24656
+, -7.29871, 0.16069, 1.25000
$ CID 993 — RIGHT HTP spline axis, y = (0.046193, 0.998933, 0.0)
CORD2R, 993, 0, 7.32000, 0.00000, 0.70000, 7.32000, 0.00000, 10.70000
+, 17.30933, -0.46193, 0.70000
$ CID 994 — LEFT HTP spline axis, y = (0.046193, -0.998933, 0.0)
CORD2R, 994, 0, 7.32000, 0.00000, 0.70000, 7.32000, 0.00000, 10.70000
+, -2.66933, -0.46193, 0.70000
$
$ CID 995 — ELEVATOR hinge axis.  AESURF takes the hinge as the CID *y*-axis
$ (same convention as the SPLINE2 spline axis, and as HA144A's canard CID 1),
$ so y must run spanwise along the straight two-thirds-chord line x = 7.60.
$ Built as z = +Z, x = +X, hence y = z cross x = +Y.  Getting this wrong is
$ silent: the ELEV column of D_jx comes out identically zero and the trim
$ Schur system goes singular with nothing to say why.
CORD2R, 995, 0, 7.60, 0.0, 0.70, 7.60, 0.0, 10.70
+, 17.60, 0.0, 0.70
$
