$ --------------------------------------------------------- CONTROL SURFACE
$ ELEV is a PART-CHORD elevator: the aft two of the six chordwise box rows on
$ each HTP panel, i.e. the aft third of the chord, hinged on the straight
$ two-thirds-chord line x = 7.60 (CID 995 x-axis).  Box IDs are
$ EID + i_span*NCHORD + j_chord with NCHORD = 6, so the hinged rows are
$ j_chord = 4 and 5: 3000+6s+{4,5} and 4000+6s+{4,5} for s = 0..7.
AESURF, 505, ELEV, 995, 3100
AELIST, 3100, 3004, 3005, 3010, 3011, 3016, 3017, 3022
+, 3023, 3028, 3029, 3034, 3035, 3040, 3041, 3046
+, 3047
+, 4004, 4005, 4010, 4011, 4016, 4017, 4022, 4023
+, 4028, 4029, 4034, 4035, 4040, 4041, 4046, 4047
$
$ ================================================================ TRIM CARDS
$ Cruise condition: q = 2334 Pa at sea level (rho = 1.225) gives
$ V = sqrt(2q/rho) = 61.7 m/s = 120 kt.  M = 0.18 in reality; the deck runs
$ M = 0.0 (incompressible) because the section data below is tabulated there
$ and the Prandtl-Glauert factor at M = 0.18 is 1.016 — inside the noise.
$
$ URDD3 = -g is the HA144A sign convention: RCSID 990 is z-DOWN, so a 1g
$ level pull is a -9.81 vertical acceleration in that frame.
$
$ RHOREF (sbeam extension) is the freestream density.  It takes no part in
$ the static trim; it exists only so V = sqrt(2q/rho) is available to the
$ transient maneuver rate terms in the MLOADS driver.
$
$ TRIM 1 — 1g cruise.  Expected CL = W/(q*S) = 1081*9.81/(2334*16.24) = 0.280.
TRIM, 1, 0.0, 2334.0, RHOREF, 1.225, PITCH, 0.0
+, URDD3, -9.81, URDD5, 0.0
$
$ TRIM 2 — 2.5g symmetric pull-up at the same q.  A steady pull-up is a
$ constant pitch RATE as well as a load factor: the nondimensional rate is
$ PITCH = q_pitch*c/(2V) with q_pitch = (n-1)*g/V, i.e.
$   PITCH = (n-1)*g*c/(2V^2) = 1.5*9.81*1.4707/(2*3810.2) = 2.8402e-3.
$ Leaving PITCH at 0 would trim a 2.5g *pull* with no *rotation* — a
$ different (and unphysical) manoeuvre, and it would drop the tail damping
$ contribution that sets the real elevator-per-g gradient.
TRIM, 2, 0.0, 2334.0, RHOREF, 1.225, PITCH, 2.8402-3
+, URDD3, -24.525, URDD5, 0.0
$
$ ============================================ TRANSIENT MANEUVER (MLOADS)
$ Phase G0 quasi-steady, open-loop, restrained-l-set Newmark-beta run.  Used
$ by cessna210_flagship_mloads.bdf; inert in the other two drivers.
$
$ Driver: IC = MLDTRIM 1, window = MLDTIME 1, commands = MLDCOMD 1,
$ ASCII print = MLDPRNT 1.
MLOADS, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1
$ Initial condition = the static 1g cruise trim (TRIM 1).
MLDTRIM, 1, 1
$ Integrate 0 -> 1.0 s at dt = 0.005 s, output every 0.05 s (21 samples).
MLDTIME, 1, 0.0, 1.0, 0.005, 0.05
$ Pilot command: ELEV follows TABLED1 300.  Labels not listed hold trim.
MLDCOMD, 1, ELEV, 300
$ ASCII time-history print.
MLDPRNT, 1
$
$ MLDCOMD TABLES ARE ABSOLUTE, NOT INCREMENTAL.  The table below therefore
$ STARTS at the trimmed elevator angle and ramps up from there — it is not a
$ perturbation about the trim.  That makes the deck a TWO-PASS authoring job:
$ run cessna210_flagship_trim.bdf first, read the trimmed ELEV out of the
$ SUBCASE 1 f06 block, then write it in here.  Start the table at 0.0 instead
$ and the airplane takes a 3.8 deg step elevator input at t = 0.
$
$ Trimmed 1g ELEV = -0.06599247 rad (-3.78108 deg).  Nose-up pull: ramp +2 deg
$ (+0.03490659 rad) over 0.3 s to -0.03108589 rad, then hold to 1.0 s.
$ Carried to 8 decimals deliberately: the transient reads t = 0 straight off
$ this table, so the authored precision here IS the precision to which the
$ initial condition reproduces the trim.  Test T5 re-solves the trim and
$ compares, so a stale number fails the suite rather than quietly producing a
$ slightly-wrong transient.
$
$ CASE-SPECIFIC.  Because the table is absolute it is also tied to the mass
$ case it was authored for.  Selecting a MASSSET alongside MLOADS shifts the
$ trimmed elevator, so this first point is no longer that case's equilibrium
$ and the run starts with a step input (see test T6).
TABLED1, 300
+, 0.0, -0.06599247, 0.3, -0.03108589, 1.0, -0.03108589, ENDT
$
